WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.012
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:18.326
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:18.326 --> 00:00:19.158
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:19.158 --> 00:00:29.987
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:00:29.987 --> 00:00:32.125
&gt;&gt; Okay, [FOREIGN], Malala.

00:00:32.125 --> 00:00:33.627
&gt;&gt; [FOREIGN] Thank you.

00:00:33.627 --> 00:00:37.798
&gt;&gt; Clearly, the GSP is very
excited to have you here today.

00:00:37.798 --> 00:00:40.785
Before we start, I want to say
a personal thank you, Malala.

00:00:40.785 --> 00:00:46.700
As a fellow Pakistani, a brother to two
sisters, a son to a mother back in Karachi

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:52.278
is I'm humbled and filled with gratitude
to share the stage with you today.

00:00:52.278 --> 00:00:55.034
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much and
I'm also honored to be here.

00:00:55.034 --> 00:00:58.209
Thank you so much to everyone for
your presence and I'm so

00:00:58.209 --> 00:01:00.274
excited to have this conversation.

00:01:00.274 --> 00:01:04.154
&gt;&gt; Great, there are a lot of important
topics we have to cover today.

00:01:04.154 --> 00:01:05.740
We don't have too much time.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:09.004
I want to start with one,
which is very important to the both of us.

00:01:09.004 --> 00:01:09.537
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:01:09.537 --> 00:01:10.723
&gt;&gt; And that's Cricket.

00:01:10.723 --> 00:01:11.554
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:01:11.554 --> 00:01:16.144
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Have you been

00:01:16.144 --> 00:01:18.166
following the World Cup in the last week?

00:01:18.166 --> 00:01:23.682
&gt;&gt; Yes, I always follow Cricket and
I even would watch any league,

00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:26.950
any match that is to do with Cricket and

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.446
I watched as much as well
that are five days long, but

00:01:31.446 --> 00:01:35.840
T20 is the shortest in
the international ones and

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.450
Pakistan is doing really well this time.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.498
It started with India,
then you beat them, then New Zealand.

00:01:43.498 --> 00:01:44.949
Again, we won.

00:01:44.949 --> 00:01:48.427
And today, it was Afghanistan,
we w on again.

00:01:48.427 --> 00:01:51.976
I think in the last two overs,
a lot of people had given up and

00:01:51.976 --> 00:01:55.614
they thought Pakistan was going to lose,
but I had full faith.

00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:59.735
A Pakistan was going to win today and
my intuition is never wrong.

00:01:59.735 --> 00:02:01.557
&gt;&gt; Wow, there you go.

00:02:01.557 --> 00:02:04.399
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Great, so

00:02:04.399 --> 00:02:07.108
let's perhaps start with Pakistan.

00:02:07.108 --> 00:02:12.101
Before you were even ten years old back
in Swat, you were allowed advocate in

00:02:12.101 --> 00:02:16.330
your community for close education and
drills being in school.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:20.408
That's not I would say typical of
a ten-year old girl in Pakistan.

00:02:20.408 --> 00:02:23.213
What made you do that at that early age?

00:02:23.213 --> 00:02:27.305
&gt;&gt; I think people often time
hear about my story and

00:02:27.305 --> 00:02:31.194
they are surprised that
I was speaking out for

00:02:31.194 --> 00:02:37.248
my right to education at age 10,
11 and it is an unusual story but.

00:02:37.248 --> 00:02:44.788
Also, this was the case, because what I
was experiencing was unusual for girls.

00:02:44.788 --> 00:02:47.637
And that was because at that time,

00:02:47.637 --> 00:02:52.661
the Pakistani Taliban had
entered Swat Valley in 2007.

00:02:52.661 --> 00:02:58.117
They started spreading this ideology
of establishing the so-called

00:02:58.117 --> 00:03:04.311
Islamic state and they wanted to bring
in their own so-called Sharia system and

00:03:04.311 --> 00:03:09.136
they wanted to like Pakistan
was already an Islamic country.

00:03:09.136 --> 00:03:15.015
So how do you make another Islamic
country inside in an Islamic country?

00:03:15.015 --> 00:03:20.149
But then along with that, they also
had this narrative against women and

00:03:20.149 --> 00:03:23.021
they carry a very patriarchal mindset.

00:03:23.021 --> 00:03:26.486
As soon as they entered,
they banned women from going to markets.

00:03:26.486 --> 00:03:31.096
They banned women from having any
profession from going to work.

00:03:31.096 --> 00:03:35.787
They will not allow them to go
outside their houses without wearing

00:03:35.787 --> 00:03:40.394
a full shuttlecock burqa and
you had to have a custodian with you,

00:03:40.394 --> 00:03:43.096
it could even be your little brother.

00:03:43.096 --> 00:03:47.916
And I remember my mom or me or any woman
like going outside to see a doctor or

00:03:47.916 --> 00:03:52.815
somebody and they would have these
little boys with them and I would always

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:58.058
question, how's this little boy going
to protect me, right, they can't.

00:03:58.058 --> 00:03:59.616
Instead, I'm protecting them.

00:03:59.616 --> 00:04:02.193
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But at that time,

00:04:02.193 --> 00:04:06.800
they also announced a ban on girls
education and I could not go to school.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.534
My friends could not go to school.

00:04:08.534 --> 00:04:13.724
So I had no choice, but to speak out for
my right to go to school.

00:04:13.724 --> 00:04:17.752
&gt;&gt; And I want to touch on the role
your father played, the Godin.

00:04:17.752 --> 00:04:20.431
You've spoken about him previously.

00:04:20.431 --> 00:04:26.046
How much of an influence was he in
that upbringing at that early age,

00:04:26.046 --> 00:04:29.405
especially when it comes to education?

00:04:29.405 --> 00:04:30.943
&gt;&gt; I'm really proud of my dad.

00:04:30.943 --> 00:04:35.610
He is a proud feminist and
he saw that his own five

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:39.931
sisters could not get their education, and

00:04:39.931 --> 00:04:44.622
they did not have access
to a future that he had.

00:04:44.622 --> 00:04:49.288
And he knew that his life was different,
because he was a boy nothing else.

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:55.158
Gender played a role in deciding
the destinies of of the siblings and

00:04:55.158 --> 00:05:00.827
he was really passionate about
bringing equality into society.

00:05:00.827 --> 00:05:03.654
So he decided that when
he has his own children,

00:05:03.654 --> 00:05:07.977
he would ensure that they're not
discriminated based on their gender.

00:05:07.977 --> 00:05:13.442
And he has always loved me and
he has been a proud that

00:05:13.442 --> 00:05:18.399
he has encouraged me to believe in myself,
and

00:05:18.399 --> 00:05:24.905
I always tell people that my
story is not an exception story.

00:05:24.905 --> 00:05:28.418
It could have been the story of
any girl if their fathers and

00:05:28.418 --> 00:05:31.227
their brothers had allowed
them to speak out.

00:05:31.227 --> 00:05:34.020
Many were stopped by their family members.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.301
Many were stopped by men in the society.

00:05:36.301 --> 00:05:40.700
What's different in my story is
that my father did not stop me.

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:46.978
And that's what he says when you ask him,
what did you do for your daughter?

00:05:46.978 --> 00:05:49.380
He says that don't ask me what I did, but

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.504
asked me what I did not do and
I did not clip her wings.

00:05:52.504 --> 00:05:57.450
So it's a message to women and girls all
around the world that they don't need

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:01.878
any sort of superpower or anything
special to go ahead in their life.

00:06:01.878 --> 00:06:06.359
There are so many barriers in front of
them that makes it more difficult for

00:06:06.359 --> 00:06:07.454
them to go ahead.

00:06:07.454 --> 00:06:11.789
So we need to fight against those
barriers, the glass ceiling,

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:15.816
the iron bars in their way and
men have a role to play in that.

00:06:15.816 --> 00:06:20.684
So for me,
my dad has always been supportive and

00:06:20.684 --> 00:06:26.062
I have always been this sort
of I like giving advice.

00:06:26.062 --> 00:06:30.778
So I'm always there telling my dad how
things should be and what we should do.

00:06:30.778 --> 00:06:31.930
When you're like a kid and

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:35.034
that's your thing, and that's how
you talk to your family members.

00:06:35.034 --> 00:06:38.772
So I have carried it on since then,
but he always listens.

00:06:38.772 --> 00:06:40.440
He's a great dad.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:47.243
&gt;&gt; Great and just on your father, I read
somewhere that he also named you, Malala.

00:06:47.243 --> 00:06:48.980
It's a beautiful name.

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:53.152
You tell us more about
the story behind your name.

00:06:53.152 --> 00:06:57.967
&gt;&gt; So Malala means grief-stricken,
basically in Pashto,

00:06:57.967 --> 00:07:02.105
but the name has more
historical meaning as well.

00:07:02.105 --> 00:07:05.105
And it was a name of this Afghan, heron.

00:07:05.105 --> 00:07:11.050
Malalai of my one and
she came from this area in Afghanistan.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:16.007
And her story goes like this that there
was the second Anglo Afghan War and

00:07:16.007 --> 00:07:19.422
the Afghan soldiers were
losing that battle, and

00:07:19.422 --> 00:07:21.957
they were leaving the battlefield.

00:07:21.957 --> 00:07:27.036
When this young woman went to the
mountaintop and she raised her voice and

00:07:27.036 --> 00:07:31.700
told the soldiers that if you do
not die on this battlefield today,

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.631
you will live your life in shame forever.

00:07:34.631 --> 00:07:38.858
And her voice was so powerful that
all the soldiers returned and

00:07:38.858 --> 00:07:41.350
they fought that war, and they won.

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:45.832
I think it has more
meaning in our culture and

00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:51.287
she's probably the only
Pashtoon hero that we have,

00:07:51.287 --> 00:07:54.575
and who is known by her own name.

00:07:54.575 --> 00:07:57.798
And so
my father was just really proud of her and

00:07:57.798 --> 00:08:02.640
he Wanted to name me after somebody
who was known by her own name.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:06.556
&gt;&gt; Right, now I think that's beautiful
just based off the life you've lived so

00:08:06.556 --> 00:08:09.100
far, how it Mirrors Malala and
I know that story.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:14.140
So let's perhaps just move on,
fast forward a few years.

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:15.770
You're 17 years old now.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:20.767
This is late 2012, early 2013,
after the Taliban attack.

00:08:20.767 --> 00:08:23.894
You're recovering,
the months are rolling by,

00:08:23.894 --> 00:08:27.174
you're in a different country,
you're in the UK.

00:08:27.174 --> 00:08:31.226
Take us back to that time of helplessness,
of despair,

00:08:31.226 --> 00:08:34.530
you've gone through such a lot.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:37.890
How did you think about what you
wanted to do next in your life?

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:41.740
And kind of just put us in that position
and how did you think about that?

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:46.630
&gt;&gt; So when I was in the hospital,
I had no idea that I was receiving so

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:49.940
much support from around the world.

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:54.328
I was not seeing any television,
any mobile phone,

00:08:54.328 --> 00:08:59.570
and I was going under all
the health care and treatment.

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:04.550
And then one day this staff member
at the hospital brought this

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:07.230
basket of cards and letters.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:09.272
And I was opening those cards and

00:09:09.272 --> 00:09:12.761
reading messages from people
from all over the world.

00:09:12.761 --> 00:09:16.206
A letter from a five
year old girl to a letter

00:09:16.206 --> 00:09:20.244
from an eight year old person in the US or
in Japan.

00:09:20.244 --> 00:09:25.176
And I was completely amazed and surprised
that people had heard my story and

00:09:25.176 --> 00:09:29.726
they were sending their prayers and
they were sending me even gifts.

00:09:29.726 --> 00:09:35.080
From shampoo to scarves to shoes to
anything you could possibly think of.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:38.938
And I was completely amazed,
and then the staff member told

00:09:38.938 --> 00:09:43.276
me that this is just one box,
there are so many boxes there with us.

00:09:43.276 --> 00:09:44.862
You have received thousands and

00:09:44.862 --> 00:09:47.627
thousands of cards from people
from all over the world.

00:09:47.627 --> 00:09:56.147
And that's when I realized that I
can speak out for girls globally.

00:09:56.147 --> 00:10:01.080
The Taliban tried to silence me,
but they made a huge mistake.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:06.332
Because I am in a position where I can not
only speak about my right to education and

00:10:06.332 --> 00:10:10.970
for girls in Swat Valley, but
I can speak out for girls globally.

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:15.128
And since then it has been my mission to
ensure that all 130 million girls who

00:10:15.128 --> 00:10:17.663
are out of school can
have access to education.

00:10:17.663 --> 00:10:18.203
&gt;&gt; Thats good.

00:10:18.203 --> 00:10:26.606
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; So that's the mission,

00:10:26.606 --> 00:10:31.460
the 130, free, safe, quality education for
girls around the world.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:35.959
How did you come up with the idea
of a fund or a foundation?

00:10:35.959 --> 00:10:39.856
Who who helped you,
who guided you at that early age?

00:10:39.856 --> 00:10:44.369
Walk us through that process of why
the fund was the best vehicle to

00:10:44.369 --> 00:10:48.100
achieve that mission
&gt;&gt; So I'll be honest,

00:10:48.100 --> 00:10:52.955
when I started this foundation, I wasn't
really sure how these things worked.

00:10:52.955 --> 00:10:56.010
I was, I think 16, years old.

00:10:56.010 --> 00:11:00.706
I was also studying,
I was a bit behind in my studies because I

00:11:00.706 --> 00:11:04.404
had missed a few years
because of my treatment.

00:11:04.404 --> 00:11:08.413
So I had to do my homework and
I had to be in school and

00:11:08.413 --> 00:11:14.710
then I had to do these events because
I was invited to different places.

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:17.808
And then I started a foundation
as well called Malala Fund.

00:11:17.808 --> 00:11:21.855
And initially when I was asked,
what do I want focus on?

00:11:21.855 --> 00:11:27.140
And I said, on girls education,
I want all girls to be in school.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:28.330
That's what I want to work for.

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:30.860
But then they said no, no,
you have to be a bit more specific.

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:34.380
Do you want to work in this country or
that country and

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:38.620
do you want to build schools or
do you want, so many questions?

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:40.750
I was like no, I want to do everything.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:43.540
I want all girls to be in school,
I can't pick.

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:47.744
This has been the mission for
us since then, that we fight for

00:11:47.744 --> 00:11:50.480
all girls who are out of school.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:55.866
And we currently work in more than
eight countries including Pakistan,

00:11:55.866 --> 00:11:59.941
Nigeria, Brazil, India,
Afghanistan, Lebanon.

00:11:59.941 --> 00:12:04.711
And our mission is to ensure that
we work with local activists,

00:12:04.711 --> 00:12:09.571
and we support the local led
projects that address the issues and

00:12:09.571 --> 00:12:14.340
the barriers that girls face
in their pride to education.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:18.473
So it could be from training female
teachers, to changing legislations,

00:12:18.473 --> 00:12:22.095
to addressing social norms and
engaging the local communities.

00:12:22.095 --> 00:12:25.759
And the work has been remarkable
when you work with local activists,

00:12:25.759 --> 00:12:31.360
because they identify the problem but they
also identify solutions to those problems.

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:34.504
&gt;&gt; That's great,
you mentioned earlier studying,

00:12:34.504 --> 00:12:37.876
and you graduated from Oxford
last year in the summer.

00:12:37.876 --> 00:12:41.815
But you've been an activist
well before that,

00:12:41.815 --> 00:12:46.747
before Oxford, back in Swat and
since the attacks as well.

00:12:46.747 --> 00:12:49.660
What did you get from
that classroom education?

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:52.240
I believe you were at PPE at Oxford.

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:57.352
How did the classroom education over those
three years change your view on how to

00:12:57.352 --> 00:13:00.514
approach this problem and
just your approach and advocacy and

00:13:00.514 --> 00:13:03.640
activism compared to what you knew before?

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:04.434
&gt;&gt; I'll be honest.

00:13:04.434 --> 00:13:08.067
When I was studying at
university at Oxford,

00:13:08.067 --> 00:13:13.239
education was still important to me,
but it was not a priority.

00:13:13.239 --> 00:13:16.036
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because they tell you that you learn

00:13:16.036 --> 00:13:19.974
a lot from your textbooks and
from your lectures but

00:13:19.974 --> 00:13:25.940
you also learn a lot from meeting
people and socializing with people.

00:13:25.940 --> 00:13:30.132
And I will be honest I met
incredible people there,

00:13:30.132 --> 00:13:35.310
I made amazing friends and
I'm friends with them now forever.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:38.731
And I also got
the opportunity to engage and

00:13:38.731 --> 00:13:43.196
interact with incredible professors and
these amazing

00:13:43.196 --> 00:13:48.433
intellectual people whose books
I'll be reading for my essays.

00:13:48.433 --> 00:13:51.694
So it's just great to
have that opportunity.

00:13:51.694 --> 00:13:54.080
And then Oxford is just so beautiful.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:58.420
So you spent a lot of time in
the beautiful gardens and libraries there.

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:01.400
And you spend amazing
time with your friends.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:06.328
And then you go through essay crisis,
and then you're awake the whole night

00:14:06.328 --> 00:14:09.745
just to finish your essay
that you left the last day.

00:14:09.745 --> 00:14:11.643
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean, it's your fault and

00:14:11.643 --> 00:14:13.414
every week you are just depressed.

00:14:13.414 --> 00:14:16.546
Why did you do this to yourself again?

00:14:16.546 --> 00:14:22.640
But it happens and then you don't regret
having all those incredible experiences.

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:25.272
So you're like fair enough and
then you submit your essay.

00:14:25.272 --> 00:14:27.070
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And go back to sleep at 8am in

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:27.978
the morning.

00:14:27.978 --> 00:14:30.684
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm sure there are many people here who

00:14:30.684 --> 00:14:32.012
can relate to that experience.

00:14:32.012 --> 00:14:35.389
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You shouldn't be so hard on yourself.

00:14:35.389 --> 00:14:40.092
I want to go back to the fund's mission,
130 million

00:14:40.092 --> 00:14:44.906
girls who you want to get back
into school K through 12.

00:14:44.906 --> 00:14:48.308
This last few months and
the last year has been difficult for

00:14:48.308 --> 00:14:51.180
that mission,
on a number of different reasons.

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:55.330
Perhaps let's just go back
to August of this year,

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:57.370
the Taliban recapture Afghanistan.

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:00.970
How did you receive that news?

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:02.160
How did it sit with you?

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:07.047
What was your initial reaction
having already lived through

00:15:07.047 --> 00:15:09.455
that nine years previously?

00:15:09.455 --> 00:15:13.280
&gt;&gt; No one expected the Taliban to
take over Afghanistan, no one.

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:18.354
And I remember just two weeks
before the Taliban took over Kabul,

00:15:18.354 --> 00:15:24.166
I had a call with all the activists
that we were supporting in Afghanistan,

00:15:24.166 --> 00:15:26.763
our education champions there.

00:15:26.763 --> 00:15:31.420
And some of them still had hope that
Afghanistan can never fall into the hands

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:32.456
of the Taliban.

00:15:32.456 --> 00:15:39.330
And so, just like everyone else,
the whole world was shocked.

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:44.976
I was shocked as well,
to see that the Taliban were now

00:15:44.976 --> 00:15:51.009
in the whole country, and
they were ruling over people.

00:15:51.009 --> 00:15:58.690
And I think, it's again,
a gloomy time for women and girls there.

00:15:58.690 --> 00:16:01.700
And we already see the impact of that.

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:06.453
Women have lost opportunity to
walk freely to their job places,

00:16:06.453 --> 00:16:10.740
many of them have not
returned to their workplace.

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:15.952
And girls at this time, are not sure
if they can go back to schools or not.

00:16:15.952 --> 00:16:18.995
The Taliban announced,
I think 43 days ago,

00:16:18.995 --> 00:16:23.863
that boys can return to schools, but
they did not give any clear statement on

00:16:23.863 --> 00:16:27.080
whether girls can return
to their schools or not.

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:32.887
So, we started then a petition
together with Afghan activist and

00:16:32.887 --> 00:16:34.999
it's on avast.org and

00:16:34.999 --> 00:16:41.070
we are asking the Taliban to
immediately let girls be in schools.

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:45.702
We're also asking G20 leaders
in the Muslim countries to

00:16:45.702 --> 00:16:48.130
take a bold stand for this.

00:16:48.130 --> 00:16:50.414
They must protect the rights of girls and

00:16:50.414 --> 00:16:54.040
they must protect the right
to education for girls.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:57.889
Afghanistan right now is the only country
in the world where girls are not allowed

00:16:57.889 --> 00:16:58.690
to be in school.

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:05.014
&gt;&gt; And just on that issue, Malala,
sometimes it's difficult for

00:17:05.014 --> 00:17:08.832
us to visualize the 130 million
girls who are not in school.

00:17:08.832 --> 00:17:12.896
Where you're at Stanford in California,
dare I say much very much a bubble

00:17:12.896 --> 00:17:16.930
compared to the issues that were
all seen on the frontlines.

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:21.758
Can you help us understand why is this
not just a women's or a girls' issue,

00:17:21.758 --> 00:17:23.740
but an issue for everyone?

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:28.068
And this is not something that countries
in another part of the world need to

00:17:28.068 --> 00:17:28.899
worry about.

00:17:28.899 --> 00:17:32.978
But this is truly a global issue for
everyone around the world.

00:17:32.978 --> 00:17:39.433
&gt;&gt; I think firstly, we need to remember
that the Taliban government in

00:17:39.433 --> 00:17:45.180
Afghanistan right now,
it's a forceful government.

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:48.200
It's not by the choice of
the people of Afghanistan.

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:50.370
They had no say in this.

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:55.205
The Taliban have captured
those cities by force,

00:17:55.205 --> 00:18:00.969
even though they claim that
they have not fired their guns.

00:18:00.969 --> 00:18:04.769
But they still were holding
guns on their shoulders and

00:18:04.769 --> 00:18:10.551
they still had the cuffs on their
shoulders, so they are using power, they

00:18:10.551 --> 00:18:17.140
are using their military power to suppress
people, to force people to accept them.

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:22.519
But also they're denying people
their basic human rights and

00:18:22.519 --> 00:18:25.735
they're not very clear about that.

00:18:25.735 --> 00:18:31.916
They are giving very vague statements on
protecting the human rights of people.

00:18:31.916 --> 00:18:36.470
So I think this is the time that all
the world leaders who speak about

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:41.351
protecting humanity and who speak
about standing up for human rights,

00:18:41.351 --> 00:18:46.160
that they stand up for
the rights of the people of Afghanistan.

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:50.162
And it's really important for
them to protect the rights of women and

00:18:50.162 --> 00:18:54.440
girls, to protect the rights of
minorities, especially but also ensure

00:18:54.440 --> 00:18:59.370
that all the humanitarian assistance and
aid is provided to people there.

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:04.366
We know that the presence of the Taliban
has impact in the lives of people but

00:19:04.366 --> 00:19:09.180
there's also other issues like COVID and
severe drought as well.

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:13.698
So those issues cannot be denied either,
it's going to a stage of famine as well.

00:19:13.698 --> 00:19:17.560
And these are issues
that must be considered.

00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:20.464
So, this is what our advocacy and

00:19:20.464 --> 00:19:24.630
our activism is asking for
leaders to act soon.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:28.460
&gt;&gt; You mentioned advocacy activism.

00:19:28.460 --> 00:19:32.572
Typically that's work
done by the government,

00:19:32.572 --> 00:19:36.996
by the nonprofit sector,
by foundations and so on.

00:19:36.996 --> 00:19:41.010
A lot of us here will graduate from the
GSB and enter the business community and

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:43.320
the private sector potentially.

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:47.571
What is the role of businesses in
the private sector in partnering in

00:19:47.571 --> 00:19:50.040
various ways in solving this problem?

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:55.649
&gt;&gt; I think this is a question and
this is a challenge, and

00:19:55.649 --> 00:19:59.624
I don't have answers to
all of these questions.

00:19:59.624 --> 00:20:04.352
But I will say that everybody has a role
to play in ensuring support to those

00:20:04.352 --> 00:20:09.250
who do not have a voice right now, who
do not have the support that they need.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:13.610
A lot of people right now
in Afghanistan need safety.

00:20:13.610 --> 00:20:16.210
And there have been many organizations,

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:21.270
including businesses who have helped
in the evacuation of so many people.

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:26.310
So I think the top priority is to
ensure that the people in Afghanistan,

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:30.930
especially the activists who
are under threat from the Taliban,

00:20:30.930 --> 00:20:33.954
that they receive safety and protection.

00:20:33.954 --> 00:20:38.844
And we did that for our activists in
Afghanistan and for other families.

00:20:38.844 --> 00:20:42.697
And a lot of other organizations and
government officials have done that for

00:20:42.697 --> 00:20:44.310
many families there.

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:47.399
So that is essential.

00:20:47.399 --> 00:20:48.807
But along with that,

00:20:48.807 --> 00:20:53.345
we also need to explore other ways in
which we can use our expertise and

00:20:53.345 --> 00:20:58.780
resources to make education more
accessible for children in Afghanistan.

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:01.970
And I think this is something
where business community can help.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:06.897
How can we use the technology that we
have to ensure that education is given

00:21:06.897 --> 00:21:12.060
to girls in Afghanistan in other
ways rather than formal schooling.

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:15.788
And along with that,
I think still humanitarian assistance and

00:21:15.788 --> 00:21:17.390
support is needed.

00:21:17.390 --> 00:21:22.258
And businesses have the opportunity
to support the work that

00:21:22.258 --> 00:21:27.126
is happening there for
the safety and protection of people,

00:21:27.126 --> 00:21:32.020
for ensuring that they're
given their basic needs.

00:21:32.020 --> 00:21:35.429
So again there are so
many ways in which people can help but

00:21:35.429 --> 00:21:39.054
it also requires collective
effort to think through this and

00:21:39.054 --> 00:21:43.740
also engage the people of
Afghanistan in these conversations.

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:45.210
&gt;&gt; You mentioned technology.

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:50.137
I believe you recently had a partnership
announced with Apple based in

00:21:50.137 --> 00:21:51.039
Silicon Valley,

00:21:51.039 --> 00:21:55.780
tell us more about what's the vision
behind that partnership with Apple?

00:21:55.780 --> 00:22:00.341
&gt;&gt; So, with Apple, Malala Fund has been
working over the past many years and

00:22:00.341 --> 00:22:03.020
Apple has supported our projects.

00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:07.198
So it was also covering the work
that we are doing in Afghanistan and

00:22:07.198 --> 00:22:10.854
then other countries like Nigeria,
India, Pakistan and

00:22:10.854 --> 00:22:15.799
we run this activist program which is
called Malala Fund Education Activist.

00:22:15.799 --> 00:22:20.576
And we have I think more than 50 activist
in all of those countries right now and

00:22:20.576 --> 00:22:24.926
they are working on addressing
the problems that girl face in their local

00:22:24.926 --> 00:22:26.340
communities.

00:22:26.340 --> 00:22:29.809
For instance, when COVID started,
our activist in Nigeria,

00:22:29.809 --> 00:22:32.231
they conducted a research
straight away and

00:22:32.231 --> 00:22:35.969
they were looking at the impact
of COVID-19 on girls education.

00:22:35.969 --> 00:22:40.276
And they realized that girls missed out on
their education because they don't have

00:22:40.276 --> 00:22:41.790
access to digital tools.

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:46.485
They are also more likely to be
forced into marriages when they

00:22:46.485 --> 00:22:51.091
are stuck in their houses, and
they also could be supporting

00:22:51.091 --> 00:22:55.910
their families financially or
in the household chores.

00:22:55.910 --> 00:23:01.655
And so then they, our activists,
they started doing lessons through radios.

00:23:01.655 --> 00:23:05.745
So they would ensure that even if
the girls cannot attend their schools

00:23:05.745 --> 00:23:10.133
because of COVID, they're still
learning their lessons through radio.

00:23:10.133 --> 00:23:13.596
And that is the best form of
technology in those areas,

00:23:13.596 --> 00:23:17.510
other technological devices and
tools may not work.

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:21.817
And so, that is just one example of how
they are just ensuring that children do

00:23:21.817 --> 00:23:25.110
not miss out on education and
they keep on learning.

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:30.307
So Apple,
they are one of our biggest supporters and

00:23:30.307 --> 00:23:33.388
it's great to work with them.

00:23:33.388 --> 00:23:36.880
&gt;&gt; So that's an example of your
time commitments, your priorities,

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:38.179
partnering with Apple.

00:23:38.179 --> 00:23:43.177
And you're obviously, you travel around
the world, do a bunch of speeches,

00:23:43.177 --> 00:23:47.951
you're meeting students, you're also
at the grassroots level visiting

00:23:47.951 --> 00:23:50.470
refugee camps, and schools and so on.

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:56.782
As you think about your personal time,
Where do you feel your own time is

00:23:56.782 --> 00:24:02.070
best used in that fight against getting
the 130 million girls back into school?

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:05.290
What's most important for your time?

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:09.260
&gt;&gt; I think I'm always there
when my voice is needed, and

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:14.380
I was very fortunate that I got
the opportunity to visit countries and

00:24:14.380 --> 00:24:18.101
to join girls who needed
their voices to be heard.

00:24:18.101 --> 00:24:23.277
I have been to Nigeria, Iraq, Lebanon,
Jordan and I have met incredible

00:24:23.277 --> 00:24:27.797
girls who are fighting for
their right to safety and education.

00:24:27.797 --> 00:24:31.338
And many of these girls
have faced displacement and

00:24:31.338 --> 00:24:35.055
they have seen wars and
conflicts in their hometown.

00:24:35.055 --> 00:24:39.496
And they are living in refugee camps or
informal settlements,

00:24:39.496 --> 00:24:43.070
but they have not given
up on their dreams.

00:24:43.070 --> 00:24:48.845
Often time, I'm expected to inspire
them but it's the other way around,

00:24:48.845 --> 00:24:53.620
they inspire me and
everyone else who are joining us.

00:24:53.620 --> 00:24:59.369
And I remember there was one girl I met,
her name was Nigella in Iraq, and

00:24:59.369 --> 00:25:05.960
she was girl and her family were
decided that she should get married.

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:09.920
She was only 14 years old and
she was in her wedding dress.

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:14.682
And on that day, she decided that she
does not want to be forced into marriage,

00:25:14.682 --> 00:25:17.512
she does not want to miss
out on her education.

00:25:17.512 --> 00:25:22.005
So she took off her high heels and
she ran away to protect herself from that.

00:25:22.005 --> 00:25:26.377
And then later on when she came back,
she tried to convince her father and

00:25:26.377 --> 00:25:30.904
her family to allow her to be in school
and she started going back to school.

00:25:30.904 --> 00:25:35.102
But then when ISIS came into her hometown,
they had to evacuate, again,

00:25:35.102 --> 00:25:38.566
and now when I met her,
she was in this informal settlement.

00:25:38.566 --> 00:25:43.029
But she was still passionate about
learning and getting her education, and

00:25:43.029 --> 00:25:46.800
I saw that she was carrying
a dictionary with herself.

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:51.170
And I asked her, I said,
why do you carry a dictionary with you?

00:25:51.170 --> 00:25:53.858
She said,
I want to learn a few words every day.

00:25:53.858 --> 00:25:58.648
So this is the passion that girls
carry for education and learning.

00:25:58.648 --> 00:26:03.733
And despite all the difficulties
that they face from walking for

00:26:03.733 --> 00:26:08.629
long distances and not having
all the resources like books and

00:26:08.629 --> 00:26:14.109
teachers, they still are committed
to receiving their education.

00:26:14.109 --> 00:26:18.421
Nigella was incredible,
then she also came to the US and

00:26:18.421 --> 00:26:23.030
she spoke at the UN platform,
and she shared her story.

00:26:23.030 --> 00:26:25.920
So my goal is to meet these girls,
but also uplift their voices.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:30.560
So like leaders, listen to them rather
than me, and this is my goal in

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:35.640
every meeting that I go to is that
the activists and girls are with me.

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:40.350
And I introduced myself but
I'm like, let's listen to Nigella.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:44.030
Let's listen to these activists and
they have something to say to you.

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:47.151
You have been ignoring their voices,
so hear from them and

00:26:47.151 --> 00:26:51.490
they will tell you what the issues
are in this country for girls and women.

00:26:51.490 --> 00:26:55.687
&gt;&gt; That's beautiful, just hearing the
stories themselves is a lot more powerful

00:26:55.687 --> 00:26:57.700
than advocates and so on and so forth.

00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:03.810
Previously, you've also mentioned
potentially interest in politics.

00:27:03.810 --> 00:27:05.322
Is that still on the roadmap?

00:27:05.322 --> 00:27:08.684
Now that you're done with Oxford,
what's next?

00:27:08.684 --> 00:27:09.490
&gt;&gt; No, not yet.

00:27:09.490 --> 00:27:12.056
No interest in politics.

00:27:12.056 --> 00:27:17.070
I think when I said that I
wanted to be the prime minister

00:27:17.070 --> 00:27:22.092
of Pakistan at that time,
I was 12 or 13 years old.

00:27:22.092 --> 00:27:28.449
And I was really disappointed with
the role of our political leaders,

00:27:28.449 --> 00:27:34.290
because what was happening in
SWAT was just taking so long.

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:37.790
And for me it was just absurd that
no one would take any action.

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:44.048
And as days went by, and I thought girls
in SWAT Valley cannot go to school,

00:27:44.048 --> 00:27:47.170
there is a conflict,
our schools are bombed.

00:27:47.170 --> 00:27:53.141
We hear firing every night, we are having
sleepless nights, and we are under threat.

00:27:53.141 --> 00:27:57.670
And no one does anything, and
it happens for two years.

00:27:57.670 --> 00:28:01.993
So at that time, I said, okay, fine I
asked you guys you didn't do anything so

00:28:01.993 --> 00:28:05.930
I'll I'll one day become the prime
minister and I'll fix it.

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:10.986
But then I was really fortunate that I
have so much experience and I have met so

00:28:10.986 --> 00:28:17.290
many amazing and incredible people and I
have been able to visit so many projects.

00:28:17.290 --> 00:28:21.217
I know that the world is
a bit more complex, but

00:28:21.217 --> 00:28:24.510
you can bring change in many ways.

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:27.710
You don't have to be a politician
to be a change maker.

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:32.155
You can bring change in any role that
you take in your society from becoming

00:28:32.155 --> 00:28:37.780
a doctor, engineer, business person, to
a politician or to human rights activists.

00:28:37.780 --> 00:28:42.210
All of these opportunities gives us
some ways in which we can contribute to

00:28:42.210 --> 00:28:45.230
the change that we want
to see in the world.

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:47.668
So I hope to continue my activism and

00:28:47.668 --> 00:28:51.665
ensure that we see that day when
all girls can go to school.

00:28:51.665 --> 00:28:54.225
&gt;&gt; And
on the topic of change in many ways,

00:28:54.225 --> 00:28:58.190
there's this wave of young
activists in the last few years.

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:03.110
And it's very refreshing to see
the younger generation standing up and

00:29:03.110 --> 00:29:05.083
having their voices heard.

00:29:05.083 --> 00:29:09.457
Different activists, advocates
have different styles in how they

00:29:09.457 --> 00:29:13.534
engage with the issues that
they're tackling the community.

00:29:13.534 --> 00:29:16.390
How would you describe your style?

00:29:16.390 --> 00:29:18.370
Where does that come from?

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:19.560
What brings that out in you?

00:29:20.730 --> 00:29:25.300
&gt;&gt; I think for me, there are a few
rules that I follow in my advocacy.

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:31.033
One is that my voice and
my words should reflect me and

00:29:31.033 --> 00:29:34.434
who I am and what my values are.

00:29:34.434 --> 00:29:39.014
And then along with that,
it's also important for

00:29:39.014 --> 00:29:43.280
me, that whatever I say is the truth.

00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:47.647
So as long as you are speaking the truth,

00:29:47.647 --> 00:29:51.115
and as long as you are yourself,

00:29:51.115 --> 00:29:56.150
then that should be your form of advocacy.

00:29:56.150 --> 00:29:58.867
But I also think that we are all
in different positions and

00:29:58.867 --> 00:30:00.470
in different places.

00:30:00.470 --> 00:30:03.187
And some of us want to
work from the inside,

00:30:03.187 --> 00:30:06.200
some of us want to work from the outside.

00:30:06.200 --> 00:30:10.930
And I think, we all have a role to play,
and we are all helping each other.

00:30:10.930 --> 00:30:15.874
So I do not underestimate or undermine the
activism that other people are taking in

00:30:15.874 --> 00:30:19.254
different forms,
I think it's incredibly powerful.

00:30:19.254 --> 00:30:26.260
And and all the activists should just
realize that they're all on the same side.

00:30:26.260 --> 00:30:27.830
They're all on the same side and

00:30:27.830 --> 00:30:32.000
they must support each other because
that helps them in achieving their goal.

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:36.561
They have a lot more in common, and they
must not lose focus on the issues that

00:30:36.561 --> 00:30:40.450
they want to address and
the change that they want to bring.

00:30:40.450 --> 00:30:46.892
So in the education field or in other
fields when there is activism happening,

00:30:46.892 --> 00:30:50.810
I appreciate the role of
every person in there.

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:56.897
&gt;&gt; And speaking about different types
of activism and different causes and so

00:30:56.897 --> 00:31:00.520
on, The Malala Fund in your vision is for
girls education.

00:31:01.780 --> 00:31:06.106
But there are so many other issues and
justices reported in the media,

00:31:06.106 --> 00:31:10.370
some not even mentioned in the media
happening all around the world.

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:14.256
Given your voice, your platform Olala,

00:31:14.256 --> 00:31:17.656
how do you think about when
to speak up and when not to?

00:31:17.656 --> 00:31:20.526
&gt;&gt; There are some issues
that have impacted me,

00:31:20.526 --> 00:31:23.240
and that have been part of my story.

00:31:23.240 --> 00:31:26.620
And I think I should be speaking
out about those issues.

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:29.672
And that issue includes girls education,

00:31:29.672 --> 00:31:34.390
I speak out for girls right to
education all over the world.

00:31:34.390 --> 00:31:40.499
But then I also speak out about what is
happening like in parts of Pakistan and

00:31:40.499 --> 00:31:41.749
Afghanistan.

00:31:41.749 --> 00:31:45.470
Because we had also been
part of that long decades,

00:31:45.470 --> 00:31:49.750
long terrorism,
that the people have faced there.

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:52.622
Whether it's the Pakistani Taliban,
or Afghan Taliban,

00:31:52.622 --> 00:31:57.032
they have all They all
carry the same ideology and

00:31:57.032 --> 00:32:01.287
the same acts and
we all have been their victims.

00:32:01.287 --> 00:32:03.548
So I speak out about that as well.

00:32:03.548 --> 00:32:08.121
And I speak out about the issues
that impact girls education, but

00:32:08.121 --> 00:32:13.194
there are other issues as well in which
it connects to girls education and

00:32:13.194 --> 00:32:18.544
my mission but sometimes like I'm not
the best person to speak out for that.

00:32:18.544 --> 00:32:22.103
There are so many other people
who have expertise in that, and

00:32:22.103 --> 00:32:25.399
I ensure that the platform is there,
and I support them.

00:32:25.399 --> 00:32:30.261
So, there are many things which
I do not speak out about,

00:32:30.261 --> 00:32:36.036
because I just want to ensure that
my voice is for girls education and

00:32:36.036 --> 00:32:42.949
that is understood that this is my mission
and this is what I will use my voice for.

00:32:42.949 --> 00:32:44.118
&gt;&gt; Yep.

00:32:44.118 --> 00:32:46.920
You're traveling around the world.

00:32:46.920 --> 00:32:49.808
You're used to be a student as well.

00:32:49.808 --> 00:32:51.650
You're juggling a lot of different things.

00:32:51.650 --> 00:32:56.363
We see you on TV and in the news very
often, like how do you think about

00:32:56.363 --> 00:33:01.255
work life balance and just personal
health, [LAUGH] his working life?

00:33:01.255 --> 00:33:04.309
Is there even a blur or
is there a balance there?

00:33:04.309 --> 00:33:08.809
&gt;&gt; I would say it was very
difficult when I was in school,

00:33:08.809 --> 00:33:13.109
because, I used to have
homework like every day and

00:33:13.109 --> 00:33:17.710
I had to attend my classes
from 8AM till 4PM in the UK.

00:33:17.710 --> 00:33:19.638
So it was quite challenging and

00:33:19.638 --> 00:33:23.654
I remember one time I had a flight
to Norway to attend some event.

00:33:23.654 --> 00:33:27.347
And I think it was like over
the weekend or something but

00:33:27.347 --> 00:33:30.890
I was arriving the next
morning in the morning time.

00:33:30.890 --> 00:33:33.667
So I was like,
I can't miss my school days, so

00:33:33.667 --> 00:33:36.032
I took my school uniform with me [LAUGH].

00:33:36.032 --> 00:33:40.900
And then on the way back, I went straight
to my school, change into my uniform and

00:33:40.900 --> 00:33:42.991
then I was like, I can't go home.

00:33:42.991 --> 00:33:44.906
I would miss my classes.

00:33:44.906 --> 00:33:46.988
So, it was challenging.

00:33:46.988 --> 00:33:51.910
But it was important for
me that I focus on my education.

00:33:51.910 --> 00:33:53.911
But also I give time to my activism for

00:33:53.911 --> 00:33:56.569
the education of all girls
all around the world.

00:33:56.569 --> 00:34:01.172
But I had to focus on my education
because if you seek out for education and

00:34:01.172 --> 00:34:03.162
don't focus on yours,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:34:03.162 --> 00:34:04.820
&gt;&gt; You are missing the point.

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:08.688
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:34:08.688 --> 00:34:13.366
&gt;&gt; Great, are there any other day
to day activities mindfulness,

00:34:13.366 --> 00:34:18.041
is there anything you do on a daily
basis that kind of helps you stay

00:34:18.041 --> 00:34:21.869
committed to that work life
balance you mentioned?

00:34:21.869 --> 00:34:25.699
&gt;&gt; I really like, staying in touch
with my friends and talking to them.

00:34:25.699 --> 00:34:31.140
I like spending time with people
Could be like our family friends and

00:34:31.140 --> 00:34:36.771
my own school friends or university
friends, and I like playing games

00:34:36.771 --> 00:34:41.843
as well as at one point it was among us,
if anyone had played it.

00:34:41.843 --> 00:34:42.429
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:34:42.429 --> 00:34:44.971
I think we are all like
done with that right.

00:34:44.971 --> 00:34:46.155
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].
&gt;&gt; We have moved on.

00:34:46.155 --> 00:34:50.722
After that, I like playing cricket

00:34:50.722 --> 00:34:54.992
with my brothers and badminton.

00:34:54.992 --> 00:34:57.303
And I watch TV shows as well.

00:34:57.303 --> 00:35:01.447
I have seen squid game so,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:35:01.447 --> 00:35:03.827
&gt;&gt; The first episode literally
shocked me and I was like,

00:35:03.827 --> 00:35:04.635
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:35:04.635 --> 00:35:07.385
&gt;&gt; What is happening but then after
that I think you get used to it so,

00:35:07.385 --> 00:35:08.921
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:35:08.921 --> 00:35:13.718
&gt;&gt; That was good and
then in the middle I had to like stop I

00:35:13.718 --> 00:35:18.190
was like okay I need to
switch to something else so

00:35:18.190 --> 00:35:22.988
I went to Ted last so and
he made me like [LAUGH] laugh and

00:35:22.988 --> 00:35:27.927
just realize life is more positive and
easy and yeah.

00:35:27.927 --> 00:35:28.821
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:35:28.821 --> 00:35:34.909
&gt;&gt; I want to move on to there's a on to
just perhaps, criticisms that you face.

00:35:34.909 --> 00:35:39.925
And it's shocking to me personally,
but given the work you're doing,

00:35:39.925 --> 00:35:46.028
the work you've done and what you've been
through, there is no shortage of pushback.

00:35:46.028 --> 00:35:48.551
Where does that come from?

00:35:48.551 --> 00:35:52.226
Where is it bad out from that criticism?

00:35:52.226 --> 00:35:56.153
&gt;&gt; I think firstly depends
what the criticism is and

00:35:56.153 --> 00:35:58.751
if we clarify and explain that.

00:35:58.751 --> 00:36:02.562
But if somebody says that,
I should do this and

00:36:02.562 --> 00:36:06.960
not that and I haven't done enough for
this or that and

00:36:06.960 --> 00:36:11.773
sometimes I have to remind people,
I am not a government.

00:36:11.773 --> 00:36:15.878
I don't have trillions of dollars and
I don't have a central bank.

00:36:15.878 --> 00:36:16.849
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:36:16.849 --> 00:36:23.178
&gt;&gt; And, I am not that person but my goal
is to use my voice and the resources

00:36:23.178 --> 00:36:28.995
I have to convince governments
to invest in education in women.

00:36:28.995 --> 00:36:33.829
And ensure that
the resources are allocated

00:36:33.829 --> 00:36:39.183
into the right areas that
benefits our society,

00:36:39.183 --> 00:36:42.716
that empowers women and girls.

00:36:42.716 --> 00:36:47.276
But my focus is always that I am
working for girls around the world and

00:36:47.276 --> 00:36:48.956
the right to education.

00:36:48.956 --> 00:36:52.620
So I feel like I'm only
answerable to myself, and

00:36:52.620 --> 00:36:57.865
the people I have made commitment to
ensure that we make a better world.

00:36:57.865 --> 00:37:00.503
Other than that,
people can say anything right.

00:37:00.503 --> 00:37:06.174
And I think also in on social media,
you see comments and things like that.

00:37:06.174 --> 00:37:10.311
And you would see like 20 positive
ones and just one negative one and

00:37:10.311 --> 00:37:13.173
then all our focus is on
the negative one right.

00:37:13.173 --> 00:37:15.034
We dismiss all the positive ones.

00:37:15.034 --> 00:37:20.374
But I think it's important to remember
that sometimes the social media

00:37:20.374 --> 00:37:25.274
world does not actually represent
the real world that we live in.

00:37:25.274 --> 00:37:30.299
And I will say that, so
far I have not seen anyone in person

00:37:30.299 --> 00:37:35.667
who has been hateful to me,
who has said anything negative.

00:37:35.667 --> 00:37:41.520
They have always been supportive and
even if they have been critical,

00:37:41.520 --> 00:37:44.703
they have done it in a very polite way.

00:37:44.703 --> 00:37:46.563
So I don't worry too much about that.

00:37:46.563 --> 00:37:49.479
&gt;&gt; That's great, specifically,

00:37:49.479 --> 00:37:55.005
maybe lasering in on Pakistan,
you visited since the attack.

00:37:55.005 --> 00:37:57.349
The pandemic has obviously
made that more difficult.

00:37:57.349 --> 00:38:01.930
What is your long-term kind
of ambition with Pakistan and

00:38:01.930 --> 00:38:06.703
what you want to see there, and
the role you want to play there?

00:38:06.703 --> 00:38:11.156
I hope I can go to Pakistan more often,
and I was planning to go already but

00:38:11.156 --> 00:38:14.153
then pandemic made things
a bit more difficult.

00:38:14.153 --> 00:38:20.352
We have been working in Pakistan,
since we started Malala Fund.

00:38:20.352 --> 00:38:24.676
Our first project started from Pakistan,
and that was to announce this

00:38:24.676 --> 00:38:29.518
project which was the Education of 40
girls who were in domestic child labor.

00:38:29.518 --> 00:38:32.056
So to give them education and
they're still learning,

00:38:32.056 --> 00:38:34.168
they're still continuing their education.

00:38:34.168 --> 00:38:36.408
They are in like higher grades now.

00:38:36.408 --> 00:38:43.279
And since then, we have now done even,
more and bigger projects since then.

00:38:43.279 --> 00:38:47.466
We have started a school in
the hometown of my parents Changla.

00:38:47.466 --> 00:38:54.619
And Changla is considered to be one of
the most deprived and areas in Pakistan.

00:38:54.619 --> 00:38:58.456
It's a mountainous area, and
this is the first school for

00:38:58.456 --> 00:39:02.855
girls there where girls can
complete their secondary education.

00:39:02.855 --> 00:39:08.025
And like every year we get hundreds and
1000s of applications.

00:39:08.025 --> 00:39:13.278
And we can only pick a few students but
what you realize is that when quality

00:39:13.278 --> 00:39:18.531
education is available, when the state
of the art school is available and

00:39:18.531 --> 00:39:22.708
when parents feel safe for
their children to go to school.

00:39:22.708 --> 00:39:27.281
Then the social norms and,
all that like stigma has been

00:39:27.281 --> 00:39:32.442
associated with education for
girls changes really quickly,

00:39:32.442 --> 00:39:37.619
and we see so many families
sending their daughters to school.

00:39:37.619 --> 00:39:41.580
And so the positive chain
has already been seen, and

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:46.812
along with that we do our advocacy
through to the champions project.

00:39:46.812 --> 00:39:49.310
And in Pakistan we have
many many champions and

00:39:49.310 --> 00:39:52.324
activists there who we
are supporting in their world for

00:39:52.324 --> 00:39:57.410
girls secondary education What do you
miss most about living in Pakistan?

00:39:57.410 --> 00:40:00.627
&gt;&gt; I miss my home,
I miss I miss Swat Valley and Shangri-La,

00:40:00.627 --> 00:40:03.430
those are very beautiful places.

00:40:03.430 --> 00:40:06.540
And I miss my time with my friends.

00:40:06.540 --> 00:40:09.940
Yeah, and the food as well of course,
you know.

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:14.765
I live in the UK so
[LAUGH] I always miss the food.

00:40:14.765 --> 00:40:20.980
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Got it.

00:40:20.980 --> 00:40:25.270
So just before we move on to
the audience questions, the mission and

00:40:25.270 --> 00:40:28.980
the vision Malala,
that you've taken on, is vast.

00:40:28.980 --> 00:40:31.179
It's 130 million girls.

00:40:31.179 --> 00:40:35.204
We hope it happens as fast as
possible albeit to multiple years and

00:40:35.204 --> 00:40:37.189
it's fairly long term vision.

00:40:37.189 --> 00:40:40.974
How do you stay motivated
on a day to day basis,

00:40:40.974 --> 00:40:44.468
because in a business sense
you can win a deal and

00:40:44.468 --> 00:40:48.853
kind of see very tangible
success on a regular basis.

00:40:48.853 --> 00:40:50.870
This is more long term.

00:40:50.870 --> 00:40:53.260
How do you stay motivated
on a daily basis?

00:40:54.600 --> 00:40:58.950
&gt;&gt; I think that inaction
has worse consequences,

00:40:58.950 --> 00:41:05.269
things remain as they are, so
keeping your work going is important.

00:41:05.269 --> 00:41:10.111
So staying active is important for me,
and I believe that the work that we have

00:41:10.111 --> 00:41:14.802
done and the work that, people in
the past have done for women's rights and

00:41:14.802 --> 00:41:18.601
girls education in the in the work
that all organizations and

00:41:18.601 --> 00:41:23.820
everyone is doing has brought us to
the stage and has helped us see progress.

00:41:23.820 --> 00:41:26.786
So this work needs to continue and
we need to accelerate it and

00:41:26.786 --> 00:41:29.246
we need to make it better and
we will see progress.

00:41:29.246 --> 00:41:34.354
What's worse and what's, what I think

00:41:34.354 --> 00:41:39.470
is dangerous is us all giving up rights.

00:41:39.470 --> 00:41:42.170
We should not give up,
we should not stop this work.

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:44.240
So we have to carry on.

00:41:44.240 --> 00:41:46.040
&gt;&gt; Inaction as complicity, yes.

00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:52.942
The theme for our View from the Top
Speaker Series is beyond expectations,

00:41:52.942 --> 00:41:56.248
and we like to ask all our speakers,
Malala,

00:41:56.248 --> 00:42:01.295
what motivates you as a 24-year-old
girl from Swat, Pakistan,

00:42:01.295 --> 00:42:05.490
to grow beyond the expectations
of a girl from Pakistan?

00:42:09.372 --> 00:42:16.011
&gt;&gt; I am very ambitious and
I was like that in my childhood as well,

00:42:16.011 --> 00:42:20.225
and I would always question society and

00:42:20.225 --> 00:42:26.215
the world and
why it could not be better than it is.

00:42:26.215 --> 00:42:29.176
Why do we see equality,
inequality with our own eyes and

00:42:29.176 --> 00:42:30.758
don't do anything about it?

00:42:30.758 --> 00:42:35.360
Why do we see that so
many people live in extreme poverties?

00:42:35.360 --> 00:42:37.655
Why can't we change that?

00:42:37.655 --> 00:42:41.114
Why is it that millions of
girls are out of school and

00:42:41.114 --> 00:42:43.454
nobody does anything about that?

00:42:43.454 --> 00:42:47.572
So I end, but then on the other hand,
I also remain ambitious,

00:42:47.572 --> 00:42:52.300
that we can change that,
we can see that change in our own life.

00:42:52.300 --> 00:42:58.238
And so this is something that I have
taken with me from my childhood,

00:42:58.238 --> 00:43:02.180
is that you have to remain ambitious.

00:43:02.180 --> 00:43:06.255
And, you have to be a bit more optimistic,
so

00:43:06.255 --> 00:43:11.192
I remain optimistic and
ambitious in my goals to see all

00:43:11.192 --> 00:43:15.376
girls in school in my lifetime,
&gt;&gt; Inshallah.

00:43:15.376 --> 00:43:16.578
&gt;&gt; Inshallah.

00:43:16.578 --> 00:43:19.520
&gt;&gt; Great,
let's open it up to audience questions.

00:43:19.520 --> 00:43:27.110
I believe we have one.

00:43:27.110 --> 00:43:30.245
&gt;&gt; Hi Mahala, my name is Talia Han and
I'm an MBA one here.

00:43:30.245 --> 00:43:34.430
As a fellow Pakistani woman it is
incredibly inspiring to see you here.

00:43:34.430 --> 00:43:35.790
Thank you so much for coming here.

00:43:35.790 --> 00:43:39.687
My question is as someone who has
personally navigated the challenges

00:43:39.687 --> 00:43:43.252
of a patriarchal society,
I know there are millions of girls and

00:43:43.252 --> 00:43:46.224
women back home who personally
faced restrictions,

00:43:46.224 --> 00:43:50.979
whether it's access to education, whether
it's professional opportunities, or

00:43:50.979 --> 00:43:54.303
even simply to have an independent
opinion of themselves.

00:43:54.303 --> 00:43:58.764
On a more personal basis, what is your
advice to them in terms of keeping hope

00:43:58.764 --> 00:44:01.030
alive for a more freer future?

00:44:01.030 --> 00:44:04.166
What was the thing for you that
allowed you to continue staying so

00:44:04.166 --> 00:44:05.979
resilient in your difficult times?

00:44:07.130 --> 00:44:08.440
&gt;&gt; Thank you for that question.

00:44:09.730 --> 00:44:11.660
So I'll share one story.

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:17.270
When I started speaking out in my
activism, I was only ten years old,

00:44:17.270 --> 00:44:22.394
but then as I was growing older and
I was at age 13 and 14, and

00:44:22.394 --> 00:44:28.780
especially in our society in the north
of Pakistan, it could become a taboo for

00:44:28.780 --> 00:44:33.421
a girl to be on a TV screen and
speak out to media publicly,

00:44:33.421 --> 00:44:39.580
not cover her face, and
some families may completely oppose that.

00:44:39.580 --> 00:44:44.770
And I remember like one time, my dad
asked my cousin to take me to this press

00:44:44.770 --> 00:44:49.545
conference area and my cousin just
felt extremely uncomfortable,

00:44:49.545 --> 00:44:53.313
and then he told my dad that
Malala should not speak and

00:44:53.313 --> 00:44:57.300
my dad told him that this
was none of his business.

00:44:57.300 --> 00:45:00.331
He should not be deciding anything for
his daughter, and

00:45:00.331 --> 00:45:03.940
that everything that Malala
does should be her own choice.

00:45:03.940 --> 00:45:09.288
So, but today, that same cousin is
the biggest supporter of my cause,

00:45:09.288 --> 00:45:13.388
and he's actually involved in in the work,
and he is so

00:45:13.388 --> 00:45:18.940
passionate about girls education,
and he supports me in my work.

00:45:18.940 --> 00:45:27.630
So initially,
you would see this unease among people.

00:45:27.630 --> 00:45:32.577
You might even hear hateful comments,
or you might hear people feeling

00:45:32.577 --> 00:45:36.701
just very uncomfortable to see
women in different roles and

00:45:36.701 --> 00:45:40.907
in different positions, and
women having a loud voice, and

00:45:40.907 --> 00:45:45.380
women being at the table, but
with time they'll get used to it.

00:45:45.380 --> 00:45:49.921
And in a few years like before we know it,
things have already changed and

00:45:49.921 --> 00:45:53.670
things already become like customs and
new norms.

00:45:53.670 --> 00:45:58.290
So it's important for
us to remain ambitious and committed, and

00:45:58.290 --> 00:46:03.246
it's, consistency is really important,
so we have to stick to it and

00:46:03.246 --> 00:46:08.040
ensure that we carry this work on and
hopefully things will change.

00:46:08.040 --> 00:46:11.350
We're already seeing so
much progress in Pakistan,

00:46:11.350 --> 00:46:15.614
we have women in the parliament and
we have women in different jobs and

00:46:15.614 --> 00:46:20.640
in different sectors and hopefully
it will get better, it will improve.

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:25.666
And a lot of women are speaking out
about women's safety at work and

00:46:25.666 --> 00:46:30.245
in other spaces, and
speaking out against harassment, and

00:46:30.245 --> 00:46:34.644
sexual harassment, so
there is this campaign going on for

00:46:34.644 --> 00:46:38.810
Women's Equality in Pakistan and
that gives us hope.

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:42.420
&gt;&gt; Next question.

00:46:42.420 --> 00:46:44.796
Here.

00:46:44.796 --> 00:46:48.930
&gt;&gt; Hi Malala, I'm Bruno,
an MBA one here from Stanford GSB.

00:46:48.930 --> 00:46:52.423
So first of all, I would like to
thank you for sharing your thoughts,

00:46:52.423 --> 00:46:54.000
your brave story.

00:46:54.000 --> 00:46:57.950
Definitely motivate us to make
the world a better place.

00:46:57.950 --> 00:47:02.858
Well, I'm from developing country in Latin
America where education is not a right,

00:47:02.858 --> 00:47:07.430
is a privilege, and I feel very
privileged to be here at Stanford.

00:47:07.430 --> 00:47:08.796
As I know many boys and girls,

00:47:08.796 --> 00:47:12.410
they didn't have an opportunity to
go to a proper elementary school.

00:47:12.410 --> 00:47:13.640
You might heard my country,
that is Brazil.

00:47:14.750 --> 00:47:18.053
So I'd like to ask you,
what are your thoughts about our

00:47:18.053 --> 00:47:21.920
role as students from top
universities like Stanford, Oxford,

00:47:21.920 --> 00:47:25.950
to minimize the gap between basic
education across the countries?

00:47:27.590 --> 00:47:28.830
&gt;&gt; Thank you for that question.

00:47:28.830 --> 00:47:33.570
I think you are all the future of
this world, you're all the future of

00:47:33.570 --> 00:47:38.559
your countries and I would say that
remain ambitious about your goals and

00:47:38.559 --> 00:47:45.030
you can bring change in your countries,
you can bring change in your communities.

00:47:45.030 --> 00:47:47.989
And you already might
be a role model to so

00:47:47.989 --> 00:47:52.280
many young kids who may not
have thought about themselves.

00:47:53.440 --> 00:47:58.360
Completing their school going to
university and taking a role in society.

00:47:58.360 --> 00:48:03.442
So, you are already giving hope to so
many people, so many women, especially

00:48:03.442 --> 00:48:08.910
here who could be coming from developing
countries or from difficult backgrounds.

00:48:08.910 --> 00:48:12.732
They're giving hope to so
many women and girls and

00:48:12.732 --> 00:48:17.743
when a child sees somebody else
in their dress, in their outfit.

00:48:17.743 --> 00:48:21.154
And when they see somebody
who looks like them,

00:48:21.154 --> 00:48:25.410
they can imagine themselves
in that person's role.

00:48:25.410 --> 00:48:27.993
So, you are already giving hope to so
many.

00:48:27.993 --> 00:48:32.220
&gt;&gt; You've got a question?

00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:33.611
&gt;&gt; Yeah, hi, Malala.

00:48:33.611 --> 00:48:36.901
So my name is [INAUDIBLE],
I'm an MBA two, and

00:48:36.901 --> 00:48:41.355
you may not remember this, but
we met a long, long time ago.

00:48:41.355 --> 00:48:42.775
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Do you remember?

00:48:42.775 --> 00:48:45.516
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, in all fairness reason,

